1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,283
Sweetheart, you go to the mall,

2
00:00:02,283 --> 00:00:04,345
or you push a button on your

3
00:00:04,345 --> 00:00:06,333
phone and a box shows up on

4
00:00:06,333 --> 00:00:07,070
the porch.

5
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:08,569
When I was sixteen,

6
00:00:08,569 --> 00:00:10,929
there was one store on Main Street

7
00:00:10,929 --> 00:00:13,636
that had everything in the world inside

8
00:00:13,636 --> 00:00:13,844
it.

9
00:00:14,024 --> 00:00:15,719
And I worked there.

10
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:18,035
Let me walk you through the front

11
00:00:18,035 --> 00:00:18,941
door of the F.

12
00:00:19,121 --> 00:00:20,259
W.

13
00:00:20,439 --> 00:00:23,274
Woolworth Company, Mansfield,

14
00:00:23,274 --> 00:00:25,327
Ohio, nineteen sixty.

15
00:00:25,677 --> 00:00:28,277
People called it the five and dime.

16
00:00:28,457 --> 00:00:30,956
When Mister Woolworth started it,

17
00:00:30,956 --> 00:00:33,227
back in eighteen seventy-nine,

18
00:00:33,227 --> 00:00:36,180
everything in the store cost five cents

19
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:37,165
or ten cents.

20
00:00:37,345 --> 00:00:38,175
By my time,

21
00:00:38,175 --> 00:00:40,288
prices had gone up a little,

22
00:00:40,288 --> 00:00:41,722
but the name stuck.

23
00:00:41,902 --> 00:00:44,804
There were thousands of them across America.

24
00:00:44,984 --> 00:00:47,434
And now there's not a single one

25
00:00:47,434 --> 00:00:47,817
left.

26
00:00:47,997 --> 00:00:49,042
Not one.

27
00:00:49,222 --> 00:00:50,302
So come on.

28
00:00:50,482 --> 00:00:52,525
I'll show you what you missed.

29
00:00:52,875 --> 00:00:54,802
First, the sign.

30
00:00:54,982 --> 00:00:56,297
Big red letters,

31
00:00:56,297 --> 00:00:59,091
gold trim, right across the top of

32
00:00:59,091 --> 00:01:00,160
the building.

33
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:02,892
The doors were heavy wood and glass,

34
00:01:02,892 --> 00:01:05,090
and they had a brass push plate

35
00:01:05,090 --> 00:01:07,358
that was worn down smooth from a

36
00:01:07,358 --> 00:01:08,351
million hands.

37
00:01:08,531 --> 00:01:10,045
When you opened them,

38
00:01:10,045 --> 00:01:11,271
a smell came out.

39
00:01:11,451 --> 00:01:13,273
I can still smell it.

40
00:01:13,453 --> 00:01:15,439
Popcorn, floor wax,

41
00:01:15,439 --> 00:01:18,260
perfume, and roasting nuts,

42
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:19,514
all at once.

43
00:01:19,863 --> 00:01:22,017
The floor was wooden boards,

44
00:01:22,017 --> 00:01:23,325
oiled every week,

45
00:01:23,325 --> 00:01:25,863
and it creaked in the same places

46
00:01:25,863 --> 00:01:26,632
every day.

47
00:01:26,812 --> 00:01:29,000
You could tell who was coming down

48
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,481
the aisle by the creak.

49
00:01:30,661 --> 00:01:33,030
The ceiling was pressed tin,

50
00:01:33,030 --> 00:01:34,045
way up high,

51
00:01:34,045 --> 00:01:37,429
with ceiling fans going round and round.

52
00:01:37,609 --> 00:01:40,589
No air conditioning in our store until

53
00:01:40,589 --> 00:01:42,079
nineteen sixty-two.

54
00:01:42,259 --> 00:01:44,742
In the summer the fans just moved

55
00:01:44,742 --> 00:01:46,848
the hot air from one side to

56
00:01:46,848 --> 00:01:47,600
the other.

57
00:01:47,949 --> 00:01:50,107
I got the job the summer I

58
00:01:50,107 --> 00:01:51,351
turned sixteen.

59
00:01:51,531 --> 00:01:52,564
Mrs.

60
00:01:52,744 --> 00:01:54,814
Lambert, the floor manager,

61
00:01:54,814 --> 00:01:56,270
looked at my hands,

62
00:01:56,270 --> 00:01:57,726
looked at my shoes,

63
00:01:57,726 --> 00:02:00,179
and asked me to count out change

64
00:02:00,179 --> 00:02:02,938
for a dollar and thirty-seven cents.

65
00:02:03,118 --> 00:02:04,274
I did it right,

66
00:02:04,274 --> 00:02:05,276
and she said,

67
00:02:05,276 --> 00:02:06,740
you start Saturday.

68
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:09,753
I was paid seventy-five cents an hour.

69
00:02:09,933 --> 00:02:11,570
Thirty hours a week.

70
00:02:11,750 --> 00:02:15,009
That was twenty-two dollars and fifty cents,

71
00:02:15,009 --> 00:02:17,230
and I felt like a millionaire.

72
00:02:17,580 --> 00:02:19,461
We wore a uniform.

73
00:02:19,641 --> 00:02:22,069
A blue smock with a white collar

74
00:02:22,069 --> 00:02:24,192
and my name on a little pin.

75
00:02:24,372 --> 00:02:25,104
Ruth.

76
00:02:25,284 --> 00:02:28,012
No makeup that was too bright,

77
00:02:28,012 --> 00:02:30,195
no gum, no sitting down.

78
00:02:30,375 --> 00:02:31,222
Ever.

79
00:02:31,402 --> 00:02:33,448
If there were no customers,

80
00:02:33,448 --> 00:02:34,282
you dusted.

81
00:02:34,462 --> 00:02:36,617
If there was no dust,

82
00:02:36,617 --> 00:02:37,027
Mrs.

83
00:02:37,207 --> 00:02:39,030
Lambert found some.

84
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:42,410
Now, the store wasn't shelves like today,

85
00:02:42,410 --> 00:02:43,223
sweetheart.

86
00:02:43,403 --> 00:02:44,773
It was counters.

87
00:02:44,953 --> 00:02:48,142
Long wooden counters with glass on top,

88
00:02:48,142 --> 00:02:50,513
divided into little sections,

89
00:02:50,513 --> 00:02:52,720
and a girl behind each one.

90
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:55,373
You didn't grab things yourself.

91
00:02:55,553 --> 00:02:58,224
You pointed, and the girl handed it

92
00:02:58,224 --> 00:02:58,758
to you.

93
00:02:58,938 --> 00:03:01,134
Thread, buttons, ribbon,

94
00:03:01,134 --> 00:03:04,521
hairpins, all in little bins with the

95
00:03:04,521 --> 00:03:05,985
price on a card.

96
00:03:06,335 --> 00:03:09,063
The notions counter was the busiest.

97
00:03:09,243 --> 00:03:12,029
A spool of thread was ten cents.

98
00:03:12,209 --> 00:03:14,002
A card of buttons,

99
00:03:14,002 --> 00:03:14,798
fifteen.

100
00:03:14,978 --> 00:03:17,191
A package of needles,

101
00:03:17,191 --> 00:03:17,718
five.

102
00:03:17,898 --> 00:03:20,345
Every woman on our street sewed her

103
00:03:20,345 --> 00:03:23,142
own clothes, and her children's clothes,

104
00:03:23,142 --> 00:03:25,798
and Woolworth's was where she came for

105
00:03:25,798 --> 00:03:27,546
everything but the cloth.

106
00:03:27,897 --> 00:03:30,135
Then there was the cosmetics counter,

107
00:03:30,135 --> 00:03:32,192
and that's where I wanted to work.

108
00:03:32,372 --> 00:03:35,580
Tangee lipstick, the orange one that turned

109
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:36,923
pink on your lips.

110
00:03:37,103 --> 00:03:39,080
Evening in Paris perfume,

111
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:40,977
in a cobalt blue bottle,

112
00:03:40,977 --> 00:03:42,479
for about a dollar.

113
00:03:42,659 --> 00:03:44,621
Pond's cold cream.

114
00:03:44,801 --> 00:03:47,668
Little compacts with a mirror inside.

115
00:03:47,848 --> 00:03:50,499
The teenage girls stood there for an

116
00:03:50,499 --> 00:03:51,824
hour after school,

117
00:03:51,824 --> 00:03:54,768
trying on everything and buying nothing.

118
00:03:54,948 --> 00:03:55,888
I know.

119
00:03:56,068 --> 00:03:57,311
I was one of them.

120
00:03:57,660 --> 00:04:00,240
The toy counter at Christmas was a

121
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:00,923
madhouse.

122
00:04:01,103 --> 00:04:02,171
Slinkies.

123
00:04:02,351 --> 00:04:05,020
Hula hoops, which had come out two

124
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:08,082
years before and every child in America

125
00:04:08,082 --> 00:04:08,945
wanted one.

126
00:04:09,125 --> 00:04:11,281
Paper dolls, jacks,

127
00:04:11,281 --> 00:04:13,324
marbles, cap guns,

128
00:04:13,324 --> 00:04:14,118
yo-yos.

129
00:04:14,298 --> 00:04:16,844
The kids pressed their noses on the

130
00:04:16,844 --> 00:04:19,100
glass until it was all smudged,

131
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:21,064
and I had to wipe it twenty

132
00:04:21,064 --> 00:04:21,937
times a day.

133
00:04:22,287 --> 00:04:24,296
Right by the front door was the

134
00:04:24,296 --> 00:04:26,500
candy counter, and it was the most

135
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:28,703
dangerous place in the store for a

136
00:04:28,703 --> 00:04:31,425
sixteen-year-old girl on her feet all day.

137
00:04:31,605 --> 00:04:34,784
Chocolate creams, peanut clusters,

138
00:04:34,784 --> 00:04:38,524
orange slices, all piled up behind glass

139
00:04:38,524 --> 00:04:40,300
and sold by weight.

140
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:43,015
A quarter pound of chocolate stars for

141
00:04:43,015 --> 00:04:43,882
twenty cents.

142
00:04:44,062 --> 00:04:46,040
The girl weighed it on a little

143
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:48,145
silver scale and poured it into a

144
00:04:48,145 --> 00:04:49,612
paper bag with a scoop.

145
00:04:49,961 --> 00:04:51,657
And the nut machine.

146
00:04:51,837 --> 00:04:54,087
A big glass case with a heat

147
00:04:54,087 --> 00:04:57,623
lamp inside, and cashews and Spanish peanuts

148
00:04:57,623 --> 00:05:00,114
turning slowly under the light.

149
00:05:00,294 --> 00:05:03,037
That's what made the whole store smell

150
00:05:03,037 --> 00:05:03,615
so good.

151
00:05:03,795 --> 00:05:05,750
They put it by the door on

152
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:07,255
purpose, sweetheart.

153
00:05:07,435 --> 00:05:09,617
Mister Woolworth was no fool.

154
00:05:09,797 --> 00:05:11,837
You walked in for a spool of

155
00:05:11,837 --> 00:05:14,241
thread, and you walked out with a

156
00:05:14,241 --> 00:05:15,626
bag of hot cashews.

157
00:05:15,976 --> 00:05:17,494
And in the back,

158
00:05:17,494 --> 00:05:19,772
sweetheart, we had pets.

159
00:05:19,952 --> 00:05:20,962
Yes.

160
00:05:21,142 --> 00:05:22,628
Real ones.

161
00:05:22,808 --> 00:05:25,843
A whole corner with goldfish in glass

162
00:05:25,843 --> 00:05:27,812
tanks, ten cents a fish.

163
00:05:27,992 --> 00:05:29,806
Parakeets in cages,

164
00:05:29,806 --> 00:05:31,812
singing all day long.

165
00:05:31,992 --> 00:05:33,300
And baby turtles,

166
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:35,916
little green turtles the size of a

167
00:05:35,916 --> 00:05:38,455
quarter, in a plastic bowl with a

168
00:05:38,455 --> 00:05:39,840
plastic palm tree.

169
00:05:40,020 --> 00:05:42,497
Every child in Mansfield had one of

170
00:05:42,497 --> 00:05:44,478
those turtles at some point.

171
00:05:44,829 --> 00:05:46,901
The turtles were later banned,

172
00:05:46,901 --> 00:05:47,661
by the way.

173
00:05:47,841 --> 00:05:49,827
In nineteen seventy-five.

174
00:05:50,007 --> 00:05:51,747
They carried salmonella,

175
00:05:51,747 --> 00:05:54,504
and children put them in their mouths,

176
00:05:54,504 --> 00:05:56,462
because of course they did.

177
00:05:56,642 --> 00:05:59,033
Danny had one named Ike.

178
00:05:59,213 --> 00:06:01,102
It lived for eleven years,

179
00:06:01,102 --> 00:06:02,917
longer than his marriage.

180
00:06:03,097 --> 00:06:04,688
Don't repeat that.

181
00:06:05,038 --> 00:06:06,829
But the heart of the store,

182
00:06:06,829 --> 00:06:08,753
honey, was the lunch counter.

183
00:06:08,933 --> 00:06:11,568
It ran all along one side.

184
00:06:11,748 --> 00:06:13,369
Red stools that spun,

185
00:06:13,369 --> 00:06:16,148
chrome trim, a long Formica counter,

186
00:06:16,148 --> 00:06:18,387
and a mirror behind it so you

187
00:06:18,387 --> 00:06:20,316
could watch yourself eat.

188
00:06:20,496 --> 00:06:23,012
There were pies under glass domes,

189
00:06:23,012 --> 00:06:24,714
and a big soda machine,

190
00:06:24,714 --> 00:06:27,230
and a grill that never turned off.

191
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:30,506
A grilled cheese was twenty-five cents.

192
00:06:30,686 --> 00:06:32,730
A hot dog was twenty.

193
00:06:32,910 --> 00:06:34,888
A cup of coffee was a dime,

194
00:06:34,888 --> 00:06:37,525
and the waitress kept filling it for

195
00:06:37,525 --> 00:06:38,111
nothing.

196
00:06:38,291 --> 00:06:41,203
The ladies from the courthouse came in

197
00:06:41,203 --> 00:06:42,659
at noon, every day,

198
00:06:42,659 --> 00:06:43,885
the same stools,

199
00:06:43,885 --> 00:06:45,953
and ordered the same thing.

200
00:06:46,133 --> 00:06:48,302
Tuna on white toast and a cherry

201
00:06:48,302 --> 00:06:48,641
Coke.

202
00:06:48,992 --> 00:06:51,594
And here's my favorite thing in the

203
00:06:51,594 --> 00:06:52,486
whole store.

204
00:06:52,666 --> 00:06:54,772
Above the counter hung a row of

205
00:06:54,772 --> 00:06:55,383
balloons.

206
00:06:55,563 --> 00:06:58,475
Inside each balloon was a little piece

207
00:06:58,475 --> 00:07:00,160
of paper with a price.

208
00:07:00,340 --> 00:07:02,530
When you ordered a banana split,

209
00:07:02,530 --> 00:07:04,241
you got to pop a balloon.

210
00:07:04,421 --> 00:07:06,309
Inside it said one cent,

211
00:07:06,309 --> 00:07:07,724
or nineteen cents,

212
00:07:07,724 --> 00:07:09,611
or the full thirty-nine.

213
00:07:09,791 --> 00:07:12,337
Every child in the store would gather

214
00:07:12,337 --> 00:07:15,021
around and scream when somebody got the

215
00:07:15,021 --> 00:07:15,433
penny.

216
00:07:15,784 --> 00:07:17,792
I'll tell you a secret.

217
00:07:17,972 --> 00:07:19,017
Mrs.

218
00:07:19,197 --> 00:07:22,146
Lambert knew which balloon had the penny.

219
00:07:22,326 --> 00:07:24,335
And when a little boy came in

220
00:07:24,335 --> 00:07:26,897
alone, with his hands full of nickels

221
00:07:26,897 --> 00:07:28,214
and his face dirty,

222
00:07:28,214 --> 00:07:30,499
she would point at the right one.

223
00:07:30,679 --> 00:07:32,711
She never said a word about it.

224
00:07:32,891 --> 00:07:34,249
She just pointed.

225
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:36,540
Now I have to tell you something

226
00:07:36,540 --> 00:07:37,753
that's not so sweet,

227
00:07:37,753 --> 00:07:39,209
because you should know.

228
00:07:39,389 --> 00:07:40,497
That same year,

229
00:07:40,497 --> 00:07:43,525
nineteen sixty, on the first of February,

230
00:07:43,525 --> 00:07:46,628
four young Black college students sat down

231
00:07:46,628 --> 00:07:50,100
at the Woolworth's lunch counter in Greensboro,

232
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:51,208
North Carolina.

233
00:07:51,388 --> 00:07:53,407
And they asked for a cup of

234
00:07:53,407 --> 00:07:53,930
coffee.

235
00:07:54,110 --> 00:07:55,863
And they were refused.

236
00:07:56,043 --> 00:07:57,644
Because in the South,

237
00:07:57,644 --> 00:08:00,618
that counter was for white people only.

238
00:08:00,968 --> 00:08:02,396
They didn't leave.

239
00:08:02,576 --> 00:08:04,803
They came back the next day,

240
00:08:04,803 --> 00:08:06,314
with more students.

241
00:08:06,494 --> 00:08:07,725
And the next.

242
00:08:07,905 --> 00:08:10,711
Within weeks there were sit-ins at lunch

243
00:08:10,711 --> 00:08:12,746
counters in dozens of cities.

244
00:08:12,926 --> 00:08:15,538
People poured ketchup on their heads.

245
00:08:15,718 --> 00:08:17,843
They burned them with cigarettes.

246
00:08:18,023 --> 00:08:19,544
They sat there anyway.

247
00:08:19,724 --> 00:08:22,997
By July, that Greensboro counter was serving

248
00:08:22,997 --> 00:08:23,741
everybody.

249
00:08:24,091 --> 00:08:27,473
In Mansfield, our counter served anybody who

250
00:08:27,473 --> 00:08:28,549
had a quarter.

251
00:08:28,729 --> 00:08:30,517
But I'll be honest with you.

252
00:08:30,697 --> 00:08:33,407
When we heard about Greensboro on the

253
00:08:33,407 --> 00:08:35,092
radio in the stockroom,

254
00:08:35,092 --> 00:08:37,803
some of the girls said those students

255
00:08:37,803 --> 00:08:39,195
were troublemakers.

256
00:08:39,375 --> 00:08:41,326
I didn't say anything.

257
00:08:41,506 --> 00:08:42,980
I wish I had.

258
00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:45,293
Part of that counter is in the

259
00:08:45,293 --> 00:08:46,573
Smithsonian today.

260
00:08:46,753 --> 00:08:48,303
Someday you go see it,

261
00:08:48,303 --> 00:08:50,697
and you think about how brave they

262
00:08:50,697 --> 00:08:51,049
were.

263
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:54,952
On Saturdays the whole town came downtown.

264
00:08:55,132 --> 00:08:57,849
Farmers in from the county with their

265
00:08:57,849 --> 00:09:00,051
wives, boys in letter jackets,

266
00:09:00,051 --> 00:09:02,621
mothers with three kids and a list.

267
00:09:02,801 --> 00:09:05,239
The store was so packed you couldn't

268
00:09:05,239 --> 00:09:06,051
turn around.

269
00:09:06,231 --> 00:09:08,186
The register rang all day,

270
00:09:08,186 --> 00:09:11,042
that big brass National cash register,

271
00:09:11,042 --> 00:09:13,898
with the little numbers that popped up

272
00:09:13,898 --> 00:09:14,950
in the window.

273
00:09:15,130 --> 00:09:15,955
Ching.

274
00:09:16,304 --> 00:09:18,315
There was a photo booth by the

275
00:09:18,315 --> 00:09:18,650
door.

276
00:09:18,830 --> 00:09:21,082
Four pictures for a quarter.

277
00:09:21,262 --> 00:09:22,887
You pulled the curtain,

278
00:09:22,887 --> 00:09:24,230
and you made faces,

279
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:26,562
and two minutes later a wet strip

280
00:09:26,562 --> 00:09:28,611
of photos came out of a slot.

281
00:09:28,791 --> 00:09:31,660
Your grandfather and I took four pictures

282
00:09:31,660 --> 00:09:33,969
in that booth on our second date.

283
00:09:34,149 --> 00:09:35,717
I still have them.

284
00:09:35,897 --> 00:09:38,473
They're in the shoebox in my closet,

285
00:09:38,473 --> 00:09:39,832
if you want to see.

286
00:09:40,182 --> 00:09:42,586
And there was the record counter.

287
00:09:42,766 --> 00:09:45,819
Forty-fives were about sixty-nine cents.

288
00:09:45,999 --> 00:09:47,786
The boys would ask me to put

289
00:09:47,786 --> 00:09:49,828
on a record so they could listen

290
00:09:49,828 --> 00:09:51,232
before they bought it,

291
00:09:51,232 --> 00:09:53,593
and they'd listen to the whole thing,

292
00:09:53,593 --> 00:09:54,869
and then not buy it.

293
00:09:55,049 --> 00:09:56,326
Every Saturday.

294
00:09:56,506 --> 00:09:58,728
Elvis, the Everly Brothers,

295
00:09:58,728 --> 00:10:01,278
Chubby Checker doing the Twist.

296
00:10:01,458 --> 00:10:04,004
I knew every word to every song

297
00:10:04,004 --> 00:10:04,825
that year.

298
00:10:05,175 --> 00:10:07,229
Christmas was the hardest.

299
00:10:07,409 --> 00:10:09,933
We stayed open until nine at night,

300
00:10:09,933 --> 00:10:12,240
and the line at my register went

301
00:10:12,240 --> 00:10:14,619
all the way back to the hardware.

302
00:10:14,799 --> 00:10:16,433
People bought tinsel,

303
00:10:16,433 --> 00:10:18,689
glass ornaments from Germany,

304
00:10:18,689 --> 00:10:20,944
strings of big colored bulbs,

305
00:10:20,944 --> 00:10:24,056
and those little ceramic angels with the

306
00:10:24,056 --> 00:10:24,912
gold wings.

307
00:10:25,092 --> 00:10:28,082
I wrapped presents in red paper until

308
00:10:28,082 --> 00:10:29,376
my fingers bled.

309
00:10:29,556 --> 00:10:31,711
And on Christmas Eve,

310
00:10:31,711 --> 00:10:32,121
Mrs.

311
00:10:32,301 --> 00:10:34,584
Lambert gave every girl a box of

312
00:10:34,584 --> 00:10:36,795
chocolates and a silver dollar.

313
00:10:37,145 --> 00:10:40,076
I worked at Woolworth's for two summers

314
00:10:40,076 --> 00:10:41,429
and one Christmas.

315
00:10:41,609 --> 00:10:44,105
Then I went on to other things.

316
00:10:44,285 --> 00:10:45,829
But the store stayed.

317
00:10:46,009 --> 00:10:47,112
For years.

318
00:10:47,292 --> 00:10:49,391
Every time I went downtown,

319
00:10:49,391 --> 00:10:50,169
I went in,

320
00:10:50,169 --> 00:10:52,268
just to smell it and sit at

321
00:10:52,268 --> 00:10:53,201
the counter.

322
00:10:53,381 --> 00:10:54,322
Mrs.

323
00:10:54,502 --> 00:10:57,555
Lambert retired in nineteen seventy-one.

324
00:10:57,735 --> 00:10:59,192
The waitress, Marge,

325
00:10:59,192 --> 00:11:00,940
was there until the end.

326
00:11:01,290 --> 00:11:03,314
And then the malls came,

327
00:11:03,314 --> 00:11:03,821
honey.

328
00:11:04,001 --> 00:11:06,110
And then the big stores out by

329
00:11:06,110 --> 00:11:08,993
the highway, with the giant parking lots.

330
00:11:09,173 --> 00:11:12,342
Main Street got quieter every year.

331
00:11:12,522 --> 00:11:15,761
Our Woolworth's closed in the nineteen-eighties.

332
00:11:15,941 --> 00:11:18,542
I don't remember the exact day.

333
00:11:18,722 --> 00:11:20,057
Nobody does.

334
00:11:20,237 --> 00:11:22,490
That's how these places go.

335
00:11:22,670 --> 00:11:24,748
Nobody throws a funeral.

336
00:11:24,928 --> 00:11:27,214
One day you drive by and it's

337
00:11:27,214 --> 00:11:28,713
a cell phone store.

338
00:11:29,063 --> 00:11:32,154
In July of nineteen ninety-seven the company

339
00:11:32,154 --> 00:11:35,035
closed the last of its Woolworth's stores

340
00:11:35,035 --> 00:11:35,808
in America.

341
00:11:35,988 --> 00:11:37,831
Four hundred stores,

342
00:11:37,831 --> 00:11:38,937
all at once.

343
00:11:39,117 --> 00:11:41,317
It was on the news for about

344
00:11:41,317 --> 00:11:42,182
one minute.

345
00:11:42,362 --> 00:11:44,846
A hundred and eighteen years.

346
00:11:45,026 --> 00:11:45,990
Gone.

347
00:11:46,170 --> 00:11:49,249
The company is still around under another

348
00:11:49,249 --> 00:11:50,977
name, selling sneakers.

349
00:11:51,157 --> 00:11:53,240
But the five and dime is gone

350
00:11:53,240 --> 00:11:53,815
forever.

351
00:11:54,166 --> 00:11:56,371
You know what I miss most?

352
00:11:56,551 --> 00:11:58,069
It's not the prices,

353
00:11:58,069 --> 00:12:01,027
although a grilled cheese for a quarter

354
00:12:01,027 --> 00:12:02,089
would be nice.

355
00:12:02,269 --> 00:12:05,086
It's that everybody went to the same

356
00:12:05,086 --> 00:12:05,555
place.

357
00:12:05,735 --> 00:12:08,637
The banker's wife and the farmer's wife

358
00:12:08,637 --> 00:12:10,572
stood at the same counter,

359
00:12:10,572 --> 00:12:12,283
buying the same thread.

360
00:12:12,463 --> 00:12:14,286
You saw your whole town there.

361
00:12:14,466 --> 00:12:16,719
You can't buy that with a button

362
00:12:16,719 --> 00:12:17,705
on your phone.

363
00:12:18,055 --> 00:12:20,199
So the next time you're in one

364
00:12:20,199 --> 00:12:21,628
of those big stores,

365
00:12:21,628 --> 00:12:24,129
sweetheart, I want you to imagine a

366
00:12:24,129 --> 00:12:25,630
red stool that spins,

367
00:12:25,630 --> 00:12:27,989
and a balloon over your head with

368
00:12:27,989 --> 00:12:29,061
a penny inside.

369
00:12:29,241 --> 00:12:31,447
And a lady named Mrs.

370
00:12:31,627 --> 00:12:34,434
Lambert who knows which one to point

371
00:12:34,434 --> 00:12:34,668
at.

372
00:12:34,848 --> 00:12:36,555
That was Woolworth's.

373
00:12:36,906 --> 00:12:37,727
Now tell me,

374
00:12:37,727 --> 00:12:40,260
did your grandparents have a five and

375
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:41,561
dime in their town?

376
00:12:41,741 --> 00:12:43,100
What was it called?

377
00:12:43,280 --> 00:12:45,121
Tell me in the comments,

378
00:12:45,121 --> 00:12:46,426
I'd love to know.

379
00:12:46,606 --> 00:12:48,445
And come back tomorrow,

380
00:12:48,445 --> 00:12:51,563
because Grandma has a lot more stories.

381
00:12:51,743 --> 00:12:53,659
Goodnight, sweetheart.
